course: dictionary
lesson: 4347
title: Guilds: groups that share a craft
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A guild is a group of people who do the same job or craft and join together to help each other. Guild is a noun, and its plural is guilds. When you meet a new word, read the words around it, because they are its context.
explain: «For centuries most people learned jobs as apprentices, living and working with a master for years, while guilds of bakers, weavers and builders set the rules.» The clues are bakers, weavers and builders. These are all crafts, so a guild must be a group of workers in one craft who make the rules together.
explain: «Medieval universities used words from guilds: a "master" could teach, like a master craftsman.» Here the clue is master craftsman, which points to the world of trades. Try your meaning in the sentence: "a group of craftsmen who set the rules" still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
fact: A guild is a group of people who do the same craft or job and join together.
fact: In the Middle Ages, guilds of craftsmen such as bakers, weavers and builders controlled trade in towns and trained apprentices.
fact: Medieval guilds set prices, checked quality and looked after members who were sick.
fact: Today a guild can be a club or society, such as a writers' guild or an actors' guild.
fact: In online games, a guild is a team of players, and a «guild hall» is a hall where a guild meets.
exercise: «For centuries most people learned jobs as apprentices, while guilds of bakers, weavers and builders set the rules.» Who set the rules? | Apprentices on their first day | *Guilds | Kings in castles | Children at school
exercise: Which of these is a guild today? | *A club of writers that helps its members | A wooden spoon | A type of cloud | A hot drink
exercise: «Guilds of bakers, weavers and builders set the rules.» Find and copy one craft word from this sentence. | *bakers | set | rules | the
test: What did medieval guilds do for members who were sick? | Sent them to school | Ignored them | Sold them their tools | *Looked after them
test: In an online game, a group of players who join together is called a what? | Apprentice | Castle | Weaver | *Guild
exam: «In 1299 the money-changers' guild of Florence banned the new digits in account books.» What does the word guild tell you about the money-changers? | They were children learning sums | *They were a group who shared a trade and kept its rules together | They were kings who banned digits | They were students at a university
